-- Care Chart week-by-week content, Second trimester (weeks 14-26).
-- Content: Move drafted with real 5/15/30-min tiers and Reset expanded to
-- the full 5-mood-mapped format (Heavy day/A little low/Okay/Good/Really
-- good) -- both from Roop's "Expanded Move and Reset Edition" review pass,
-- 2026-07-29, which fixed the earlier reviewed edition's regression where
-- Reset had been simplified back down to one message per week (flagged to
-- Roop at the time; this edition resolves it with real, mood-specific
-- content for every week, matching what she originally asked for and
-- what's already locked into First trimester's older format). Every other
-- card (Nourish, Hydration Goal, Care for yourself, Your Corner, Support
-- Moment, Celebrate This Week, For Your Care Team, What You May Notice) is
-- unchanged from the prior "Expanded Move Edition" pass -- Roop's own
-- reviewed content throughout, verbatim.


insert into care_chart_week_content
  (week_number, trimester, theme_title, mantra, priority, journey, what_you_may_notice, move, nourish, hydration_goal, reset, care_for_yourself, your_corner, support_moment, celebrate_this_week, for_your_care_team)
values
  (14, 'second', 'A gentler beginning', 'You do not have to rush into feeling better.', 'Let returning energy arrive at its own pace.', 'The uterus is rising out of the pelvis, and nausea or fatigue may begin to ease. Your baby is practising small facial movements, though you cannot feel them yet.', ARRAY['A little more energy, or no change yet','Less nausea, lingering nausea, or new heartburn','A growing or firmer-feeling abdomen','Stuffy nose, bleeding gums, or mild headaches']::text[], '{"focus": "Ease back into movement without chasing your pre-pregnancy pace.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk slowly for 3 minutes, then spend 2 minutes rolling your shoulders and circling your ankles.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes at a conversational pace. Finish with 5 minutes of gentle shoulder rolls, ankle circles and calf stretching at a wall.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 20 minutes, then complete two easy rounds: 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 30 seconds of supported calf stretching per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Heavy or nauseated: stay near home and choose the 5-minute option. Anxious: use a steady, familiar route. Feeling energised: complete 30 minutes, but finish with energy left.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Build one satisfying plate: a protein food, a whole grain or starchy vegetable, and something colourful. Try dal, rice and a cooked vegetable; add curd if it suits you.', 'Keep water visible. Pair a few sips with every meal, snack and bathroom break.', '{"heavy_day": "Make today smaller. Sit somewhere supported, soften your face and take five slow breaths. Nothing else has to be solved during these two minutes.", "a_little_low": "Choose one gentle comfort: sunlight, music, a warm drink or a message to someone safe. Let that be enough care for this moment.", "okay": "Name one thing your body needs and one thing your mind needs. Choose the easier of the two and do only that next.", "good": "Use the steadier energy to clear one small source of friction — a cluttered surface, an unanswered message or tomorrow''s snack — then stop.", "really_good": "Capture the lift without filling every minute. Write one sentence about what feels hopeful, then leave space to enjoy the feeling."}'::jsonb, 'If skin feels more sensitive, simplify: gentle cleanse, moisturiser and broad-spectrum sunscreen. New or strong actives can wait until you have checked them with your clinician.', 'Choose one small thing you missed in the first trimester — a favourite song, a short outing or an unrushed shower — and make room for it.', 'Ask someone close to you to join a short walk or take one routine task off your list.', 'You reached the second trimester. That is a real milestone, even if today feels ordinary.', 'Bring persistent or worsening headaches, ongoing vomiting, or questions about medicines and supplements to your care team.'),
  (15, 'second', 'Make space for comfort', 'Gentle movement can be care, not a test.', 'Reduce strain as your posture and ligaments begin to change.', 'Your uterus is growing and the supporting ligaments are stretching. Your baby is moving often, but those movements may still be too light to notice.', ARRAY['Brief pulling or sharp sensations low on either side, especially with sudden movement','More vaginal discharge without strong odour or irritation','A changing centre of gravity','More appetite, or appetite that still varies']::text[], '{"focus": "Move smoothly to reduce sudden pulling and support your changing posture.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Take five slow breaths, then do 8 wall pelvic tilts and a 2-minute easy walk.", "steady": "Walk for 8 minutes. Add 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 supported side leg lifts per side and a gentle standing side stretch.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 15 minutes, then complete two rounds of 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 chair sit-to-stands and 8 supported side leg lifts per side. Rest as needed."}, "mood_adjustment": "Tender or fearful of pulling pain: use small ranges and move slowly between positions. Restless: let the walk be rhythmic. Strong: add a second round, not deeper stretches.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Make iron easier to use: pair lentils, beans, greens, eggs or meat with a vitamin-C food such as citrus, guava, tomato or capsicum.', 'Drink regularly rather than waiting to feel very thirsty. Add lemon, mint or fruit if plain water is unappealing.', '{"heavy_day": "Put both feet on the floor and feel the support underneath you. Exhale slowly six times. Your only job is to arrive in this moment.", "a_little_low": "If discomfort is colouring the day, offer yourself one kind sentence: \"This is hard, and I can move through it gently.\"", "okay": "Scan from jaw to shoulders to hands. Release one area at a time, without trying to make your whole body perfectly relaxed.", "good": "Take a five-minute screen-free pause outdoors or beside a window. Let your attention rest on colour, sound and temperature.", "really_good": "Share the good energy: send one warm message, play a favourite song or make a simple plan you can genuinely look forward to."}'::jsonb, 'Notice where clothing presses or rubs. A comfortable, supportive bra and softer waistbands can make the day noticeably easier.', 'Take a photo of something that made today feel calm. It does not need to be pregnancy-related.', 'Share one comfort change you need — slower plans, fewer late nights or more help with meals.', 'You listened before pushing. That is a form of strength.', 'Mention pain that is frequent, worsening, severe, or accompanied by bleeding, fever, urinary symptoms or dizziness.'),
  (16, 'second', 'Notice without comparing', 'There is no prize for feeling movement first.', 'Stay curious about your body without turning every sensation into a test.', 'Some parents begin to notice "quickening" around this stage; many do not until later, especially in a first pregnancy. Your baby''s movements are becoming more coordinated.', ARRAY['Flutters, bubbles or nothing recognisable yet','A more visible bump','Back or pelvic discomfort after long periods in one position','Changes in appetite, sleep or mood']::text[], '{"focus": "Build steady leg and upper-body support while balance is still comfortable.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 6 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 1 minute of slow marching while holding a stable surface.", "steady": "Complete two rounds: 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups, 8 supported heel raises and 30 seconds of easy marching.", "feeling_good": "Warm up with a 10-minute walk. Complete two rounds of 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 wall push-ups, 10 heel raises and 8 supported side leg lifts per side. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Low-energy: one unhurried round is enough. Distracted: count breaths instead of repetitions. Feeling good: use the full option while keeping every repetition controlled.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Choose a breakfast with protein and fibre: besan chilla with curd, eggs with whole-grain toast, or oats with milk/curd, nuts and fruit.', 'Fill one bottle at the start of your day and refill it as needed. Your urine should generally be pale yellow.', '{"heavy_day": "Comparison can wait. Place a hand over your heart and say: \"My pregnancy does not need to look like anyone else''s today.\"", "a_little_low": "Mute or step away from one source of comparison for the rest of the day. Protecting your attention is legitimate self-care.", "okay": "Use the 5-4-3 reset: notice five things you see, four you feel and three you hear. Return to your own surroundings.", "good": "Record a brief voice note about something that feels more manageable this week. Keep it honest rather than inspirational.", "really_good": "Enjoy curiosity without prediction. Write one thing you are excited to notice in the weeks ahead, then return to today."}'::jsonb, 'Use support where it helps: a cushion behind your lower back, comfortable shoes, and regular position changes.', 'Record a 30-second voice note: "This is what Week 16 feels like for me."', 'Tell your partner or support person that movement may be subtle and inconsistent at first — there is nothing to "perform."', 'Your pregnancy is unfolding on its own timeline, and you are meeting it there.', 'If you are worried about symptoms or movement, contact your care team; do not rely on a home Doppler for reassurance.'),
  (17, 'second', 'Support the body you are in', 'Comfort is a worthwhile goal.', 'Protect your back and pelvis during ordinary daily movement.', 'As your abdomen grows, posture and balance begin to shift. Your baby''s skeleton continues to harden and body fat begins to develop.', ARRAY['Lower-back tiredness','Round-ligament pulling with coughing, rolling or standing','Vivid dreams or interrupted sleep','More obvious breast or skin changes']::text[], '{"focus": "Practise movement patterns that protect your back during daily life.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Practise 6 slow hip-hinges with hands on your thighs, then walk for 2 minutes and stretch your calves.", "steady": "Walk for 6 minutes. Add two rounds of 8 hip-hinges, 8 wall push-ups and 8 bird-dog arm reaches at a wall or sturdy counter.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 12 minutes. Complete two rounds of 10 hip-hinges, 10 wall push-ups, 8 supported reverse steps per side and 8 standing opposite-arm reaches. Cool down gently."}, "mood_adjustment": "Sore: make this a mobility day and avoid loading painful movements. Mentally tired: use only familiar exercises. Energised: increase time, never speed or strain.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Create an easy snack pair: fruit with nuts, roasted chana with fruit, curd with seeds, or cheese/paneer with whole-grain crackers.', 'Have a drink before and after movement. In hot or humid weather, take extra care to cool down and replace fluids.', '{"heavy_day": "Lie or sit in the most comfortable supported position available. Unclench your hands and take six breaths with a longer exhale.", "a_little_low": "Ask yourself what is making the day heavier: pain, tiredness, worry or workload. Choose one practical response, however small.", "okay": "Take a two-minute \"nothing to solve\" pause. Let unfinished tasks stay unfinished while your nervous system catches up.", "good": "Use your steadier mood to prepare one comfort for later — a pillow, an easy meal, a shower or an earlier bedtime.", "really_good": "Make room for pleasure that is not productive: music, laughter, fresh air or a conversation that has nothing to do with pregnancy."}'::jsonb, 'If sleep is less comfortable, try side-lying with a pillow between your knees or under your abdomen. Choose what feels best; reposition whenever you need to.', 'Make a tiny "comfort menu" with three things that reliably help: music, fresh air, a warm shower, stretching or calling someone safe.', 'Ask for practical help with lifting, carrying or repetitive bending.', 'You made daily life kinder to your changing body.', 'Discuss persistent pelvic or back pain, pain that limits walking, numbness, weakness, or pain with urinary symptoms.'),
  (18, 'second', 'Prepare, then breathe', 'A scan is information, not a performance.', 'Go into the anatomy-scan window informed and emotionally supported.', 'A detailed ultrasound is commonly offered around 18-22 weeks to look at growth and anatomy. Sometimes every view cannot be obtained in one visit; that alone does not mean something is wrong.', ARRAY['Clearer flutters, or still no definite movement','A stretching or itchy abdomen','Occasional dizziness when standing quickly','Excitement, worry, or both about the scan']::text[], '{"focus": "Use familiar movement to settle scan-week tension.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk slowly for 3 minutes, then take 6 long, comfortable breaths with relaxed shoulders.", "steady": "Choose a familiar 10-minute walk and finish with 5 minutes of calf stretching, shoulder circles and gentle side-to-side weight shifts.", "feeling_good": "Walk, swim or use a stationary cycle for 20 minutes at conversational effort. Add 10 minutes of easy mobility: shoulder rolls, wall pelvic tilts and supported calf stretches."}, "mood_adjustment": "Anxious: choose repetition and familiarity rather than learning something new. Emotionally drained: five minutes counts. Relieved or upbeat: enjoy 30 minutes without turning it into a test.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Prep one low-effort meal for appointment day: vegetable khichdi with curd, a paneer wrap, or a lentil soup with toast.', 'Unless your clinic tells you differently for the scan, follow your usual hydration routine. Ask ahead if a full bladder is needed.', '{"heavy_day": "If scan worry feels overwhelming, name the fear without debating it. Then name who you can contact and the next factual step.", "a_little_low": "Write the question beneath the worry. A clear question can be carried into an appointment more easily than a cloud of \"what ifs.\"", "okay": "Prepare your notes, records and one key question. Once they are ready, give yourself permission to stop researching.", "good": "Picture the appointment as one step in care, not a test you must pass. Plan one gentle thing for afterwards.", "really_good": "Let excitement exist without forcing certainty. Save a small memory from this week and share it only if that feels right."}'::jsonb, 'Wear easy, comfortable clothing to the scan. Bring your records, medication list and any questions your clinic has asked you to prepare.', 'After the appointment, save one memory: a note, a scan image if provided, or simply how you felt walking out.', 'If you want company and your clinic permits it, invite your chosen support person. Tell them whether you want quiet reassurance or active note-taking.', 'You showed up for a meaningful part of your care, whatever emotions came with it.', 'Ask when and how results will be explained, what follow-up (if any) is recommended, and whom to contact with questions.'),
  (19, 'second', 'Trust the gradual changes', 'Subtle progress is still progress.', 'Build steady routines that support energy and digestion.', 'Your blood volume is expanding and your growing uterus can slow digestion. Your baby''s movements may become easier to recognise, though patterns are not established for everyone yet.', ARRAY['Constipation, bloating or heartburn','Leg cramps or restless sleep','Movement that is still sporadic','A stronger appetite or quicker fullness']::text[], '{"focus": "Break up stillness to support circulation, digestion and back comfort.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk around your space for 3 minutes, then do 10 heel raises and 10 ankle circles per side.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes. Add 8 chair sit-to-stands, 10 heel raises and a supported upper-back stretch.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 20 minutes, ideally after a meal if comfortable. Complete two rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 10 heel raises and 8 wall push-ups."}, "mood_adjustment": "Bloated or uncomfortable: choose gentle walking and stay upright. Flat mood: pair movement with one favourite song. Energetic: use the full duration but keep the talk test.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Support digestion with fibre plus fluid: oats, fruit, vegetables, whole grains, lentils and beans. Increase fibre gradually if your usual intake is low.', 'Spread drinks through the day. If heartburn is bothersome, try smaller amounts more often instead of large drinks with meals.', '{"heavy_day": "Lower the bar for the next hour. Choose food, fluids, medication as prescribed, rest or a bathroom break — whichever basic need comes first.", "a_little_low": "Say: \"Good enough is a caring standard today.\" Complete one necessary task, then pause before automatically adding another.", "okay": "Take three minutes to tidy only the space you can reach without strain. Stop when the timer ends and notice the calmer edge.", "good": "Use the steadier moment to plan one digestion-friendly meal or snack. Preparation can support you without becoming another project.", "really_good": "Turn the good mood into connection: share a meal, call someone who restores you or take a slow walk without multitasking."}'::jsonb, 'For an itchy stretching belly, use a plain moisturiser for comfort. Creams cannot reliably prevent stretch marks, and stretch marks are not a failure of care.', 'Build a three-song "reset playlist" for walks, chores or difficult afternoons.', 'Ask someone to share food prep: washing fruit, chopping vegetables or packing tomorrow''s snack.', 'A routine does not have to be perfect to support you.', 'Seek advice for severe or persistent abdominal pain, vomiting that prevents fluids, blood in stool, or constipation that does not improve.'),
  (20, 'second', 'Halfway, pause here', 'You are allowed to feel proud, uncertain, relieved or all three.', 'Mark the midpoint with care, not pressure.', 'Around this stage, the top of the uterus may be near the navel. Movement may feel clearer, while the anatomy-scan window continues through about Week 22.', ARRAY['More recognisable kicks or rolls, or movement that is still faint','A noticeably changing shape and posture','Breathlessness with effort','New feelings about visibility, identity or the months ahead']::text[], '{"focus": "Celebrate what your body can do at the midpoint, without comparison.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Take a 5-minute \"halfway walk\" and notice three things your body is doing for you today.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes, then complete 5 minutes of your favourite mobility moves from this trimester.", "feeling_good": "Walk, swim or cycle for 20 minutes. Add two gentle rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 8 supported side leg lifts per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Emotional: let movement be reflective, not productive. Tired: take the five-minute walk. Celebratory: choose the activity you genuinely enjoy rather than the one that looks most impressive.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Choose a celebratory meal that also feels good afterwards. A balanced thali, grilled fish or paneer with vegetables, or a favourite home meal all count.', 'Make hydration part of the celebration: a chilled water bottle, coconut water where food-safe and suitable, or infused water — without turning it into a target to "win."', '{"heavy_day": "Milestones can bring complicated feelings. Put away the expectation to celebrate and ask only: \"What would make today feel safer or softer?\"", "a_little_low": "Name what you have carried to reach halfway — physical, emotional and practical. You do not have to minimise it to feel grateful.", "okay": "Complete a halfway check-in: What feels easier? What remains hard? What support would make the next month gentler?", "good": "Choose one midpoint ritual that feels natural: a favourite meal, a photo, a quiet walk or ten minutes of reflection.", "really_good": "Celebrate without turning joy into a performance. Record three things you are proud of and one hope for the next trimester."}'::jsonb, 'Take a bump photo only if it feels meaningful. You do not owe anyone documentation, sharing or a particular kind of joy.', 'Write or record three lines to your future self: what you have carried, what you have learned and what you want to protect.', 'Create a small midpoint ritual — a favourite meal, a quiet photo, a walk or ten minutes talking without logistics.', 'Halfway is not "only halfway." Look at how much your body and life have already held.', 'From 20 weeks onward, contact your clinician promptly for a severe headache that will not go away, vision changes, swelling of the face or hands, upper-abdominal pain, or difficulty breathing.'),
  (21, 'second', 'Make everyday life easier', 'Small adjustments count as real care.', 'Reduce friction in the routines you repeat every day.', 'Your centre of gravity continues to shift, and baby movements may become more noticeable. Skin stretching, mild swelling and back discomfort can also appear.', ARRAY['More consistent movement, but not necessarily a schedule','Backache after standing or sitting','Mild ankle or foot swelling later in the day','Skin colour changes or itchiness']::text[], '{"focus": "Support balance, calves and back as your centre of gravity shifts.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Hold a stable chair and complete 10 heel raises, 8 side-to-side weight shifts and a 1-minute easy walk.", "steady": "Walk for 7 minutes. Add two rounds of 10 heel raises, 8 supported side steps per direction and 8 wall push-ups.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 15 minutes. Complete two rounds of 10 heel raises, 10 supported side steps per direction, 10 wall push-ups and 8 chair sit-to-stands. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Unsteady: use support and skip balance challenges. Frustrated: focus on strong, slow repetitions. Feeling capable: extend the walk rather than removing support.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Make lunch easier to assemble: roti with dal and vegetables, curd rice with a protein side, or a whole-grain sandwich/wrap with paneer, egg, chicken or beans.', 'Keep water at the place where you spend the most time. Take it with you whenever you leave home.', '{"heavy_day": "Ask: \"What is the next kind thing — not the whole plan?\" Do only that, then reassess rather than carrying the entire day at once.", "a_little_low": "Reduce one repeated frustration. Move an item within reach, ask for help or cancel one nonessential task. Small adjustments are real care.", "okay": "Take five quiet minutes to notice what your routine is asking from you. Change one detail that would make tomorrow easier.", "good": "Use the steadier mood for a practical reset: refill water, prepare a snack and clear your resting place. Then enjoy the ease you created.", "really_good": "Let the good day contain something memorable. Wear a favourite outfit, visit somewhere pleasant or make an ordinary moment feel yours."}'::jsonb, 'Choose supportive footwear and change positions often. Elevating your feet briefly may ease ordinary end-of-day swelling.', 'Wear, use or revisit something that feels distinctly like you — not just "pregnant you."', 'Ask your support person to notice one household routine they can fully own this week.', 'You improved a real day, not an imaginary perfect one.', 'Call promptly for sudden or one-sided swelling, calf pain/redness, swelling with headache or vision changes, or difficulty breathing.'),
  (22, 'second', 'Prepare without overbuying', 'You can research slowly and decide later.', 'Identify what may support your comfort before shopping for baby gear.', 'Your baby''s hearing is developing, and movement may be increasingly familiar. Your body may need more support during sleep, walking and work.', ARRAY['Stronger movement at certain times','Pelvic heaviness or lower-back fatigue','More discharge without irritation or strong odour','Changing sleep positions']::text[], '{"focus": "Build comfortable lower-body strength for walking, stairs and daily tasks.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 supported side steps per direction and 30 seconds of calf stretching per side.", "steady": "Complete two rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 10 supported side steps per direction, 10 heel raises and 8 wall push-ups.", "feeling_good": "Warm up with a 10-minute walk. Complete two rounds of 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 supported side steps per direction, 10 heel raises and 10 wall push-ups. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Heavy pelvis or back: shorten the range and stop any move that increases pressure or pain. Bored: change the music or location. Strong: complete the full routine with steady breathing.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Plan one "emergency meal" using safe staples: dal and frozen vegetables, eggs and toast, or a bean-and-rice bowl. Convenience is a care tool.', 'Use meals as anchors: drink with breakfast, lunch and dinner, then add regular sips between them.', '{"heavy_day": "Close the shopping tabs and step away. Nothing has to be researched today. Comfort and rest are valid preparation too.", "a_little_low": "Choose one decision to postpone on purpose. Write \"not this week\" beside it and notice the space that decision creates.", "okay": "Research one comfort item for five minutes. Record the useful information, then stop before one question becomes an entire registry.", "good": "Make two lists: \"may genuinely help\" and \"can wait.\" Keep both short and let function matter more than marketing.", "really_good": "Enjoy planning without urgency. Explore one idea with your support person, then finish with something unrelated to pregnancy."}'::jsonb, 'If you need it, compare supportive bras, pillows or footwear. Fit and comfort matter more than pregnancy branding.', 'Read, sing or play one song aloud. Bonding can be ordinary and private.', 'Choose one baby-related decision to explore together and one you are deliberately postponing.', 'You prepared without letting preparation take over the day.', 'Report watery fluid that keeps leaking, bleeding, painful contractions, fever, or discharge with strong odour, itching or burning.'),
  (23, 'second', 'Protect your energy', 'Rest before you are emptied out.', 'Use your better-energy windows wisely and leave margin around them.', 'Your baby is adding weight and practising movements. You may feel more physically pregnant now, even on days when energy is good.', ARRAY['More obvious kicks, rolls or stretches','Breathlessness on stairs or hills','Leg cramps, backache or tired feet','A need for more recovery after busy days']::text[], '{"focus": "Protect energy by matching effort to how you feel before you begin.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Walk for 3 minutes, then take 2 minutes for shoulder rolls, ankle circles and slow breathing.", "steady": "Choose 10 minutes of walking, swimming or stationary cycling. Finish with 5 minutes of easy mobility.", "feeling_good": "Do 20 minutes of conversational aerobic movement. Add one easy strength round: 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 wall push-ups, 10 heel raises and 8 side leg lifts per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Depleted: stop at five minutes even if the plan says more. Stressed: choose rhythmic aerobic movement. Energised: use 30 minutes but rate the effort as moderate, not all-out.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Pair calcium-rich foods with your routine: milk, curd/yoghurt, paneer, calcium-set tofu or other fortified options that fit your diet.', 'Drink before, during and after activity. Dark urine, dizziness or a racing heart can signal that you need fluids and a pause.', '{"heavy_day": "Rest before you are completely emptied out. Dim the room, put the phone aside and allow ten minutes with no conversation or decisions.", "a_little_low": "Identify what drained you most today. Ask whether it can be shortened, shared, delayed or removed next time.", "okay": "Schedule a ten-minute recovery window after the busiest part of tomorrow. Treat it as part of the plan, not a reward.", "good": "Protect the steadier energy by leaving margin around it. Finish one meaningful task and deliberately keep the rest of the hour light.", "really_good": "Spend the energy on something restorative or joyful — not only catching up. Stop while the day still feels good."}'::jsonb, 'A warm (not hot) shower, gentle foot care and loose clothing can help after a long day. Avoid heat that makes you dizzy or overheated.', 'Take yourself on a small date: tea, a balcony break, a bookstore visit or an unrushed snack.', 'Tell your support person what "protect my energy" looks like in practice this week.', 'You stopped before your body had to shout.', 'Contact your clinician for breathlessness at rest, chest pain, fainting, a racing heart that does not settle, or calf pain/swelling.'),
  (24, 'second', 'Screening is information', 'A test result is not a grade.', 'Prepare calmly for the usual gestational-diabetes screening window.', 'Glucose screening is commonly offered between 24 and 28 weeks, though timing and method vary. Gestational diabetes is influenced by pregnancy hormones; it is not a moral judgment about food or willpower.', ARRAY['More thirst or urination may occur in normal pregnancy and do not diagnose diabetes','More established movement for some parents','Rib, back or pelvic discomfort','Mixed feelings about tests and results']::text[], '{"focus": "Use regular movement for wellbeing, not to earn food or influence a test result.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Take a comfortable 5-minute walk, especially after a meal if that feels good.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes. Add 5 minutes of wall pelvic tilts, heel raises and a supported chest-opening stretch.", "feeling_good": "Walk, swim or cycle for 20 minutes. Add two rounds of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 10 heel raises."}, "mood_adjustment": "Worried about screening: choose movement that calms you; do not exercise intensely to \"prepare\" for the test. Tired after an appointment: rest or choose five minutes. Well-rested: follow your normal moderate option.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Do not "eat perfectly" for the test. Follow your clinic''s preparation instructions. For everyday meals, combine carbohydrate foods with protein, fibre and fat for satisfaction.', 'Ask whether plain water is allowed during your test and follow the clinic''s instructions exactly. Bring water for afterwards if permitted.', '{"heavy_day": "A test result is information, never a grade. Put both feet down, lengthen your exhale and choose one person you can contact for support.", "a_little_low": "Write the practical questions that reduce uncertainty: preparation, timing, results and next steps. Let the clinic answer the medical questions.", "okay": "Prepare what you need for the appointment, then close the loop for today. More searching is not the same as more control.", "good": "Plan a comfortable screening day: easy clothing, transport, something to read and food for afterwards if your clinic permits.", "really_good": "Use your steadier mood to remind yourself: seeking information is an act of care. Celebrate completing the step, regardless of the result."}'::jsonb, 'Plan a comfortable appointment day: easy clothing, something to read, and a snack for afterwards if your clinic allows.', 'Save one non-medical moment from this week — a photo, a joke, a meal or a voice note.', 'Ask someone to handle transport, scheduling or a meal on screening day if that would reduce stress.', 'You took care of your health by getting information.', 'Follow up on results and next steps. An abnormal screening result may require another test; it does not by itself confirm gestational diabetes.'),
  (25, 'second', 'Learn your baby''s ordinary', 'Connection grows through attention, not perfection.', 'Notice movement naturally without forcing a formal count unless advised.', 'Movements may feel stronger and more distinctive, but every baby and placenta position affects how movement is felt. Formal movement-counting guidance often begins later and varies by care plan.', ARRAY['Kicks, rolls, stretches or hiccup-like rhythms','Movement that is easier to feel at rest','Pelvic pressure or backache','Sleep interrupted by comfort or activity']::text[], '{"focus": "Combine mobility and strength without using movement as a way to test the baby.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 shoulder rolls in each direction and a 2-minute walk.", "steady": "Walk for 7 minutes. Complete two rounds of 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 wall push-ups and 8 supported reverse steps per side.", "feeling_good": "Walk for 15 minutes. Complete two rounds of 10 wall pelvic tilts, 10 wall push-ups, 8 supported reverse steps per side and 10 heel raises. Cool down gently."}, "mood_adjustment": "Preoccupied with movement sensations: use the routine for your own wellbeing, not to provoke or check movement. Anxious about a change: contact your care team. Feeling good: enjoy the full routine at moderate effort.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Build a portable snack kit for busy days: nuts or roasted chana, fruit, whole-grain crackers, or a safely stored dairy/protein option.', 'Pair movement awareness with a normal pause and a drink — not as a trick to "make" baby move, but as a moment to settle and notice.', '{"heavy_day": "If worry about movement is strong or something feels clearly different, contact your care team. A calming exercise should never replace medical advice.", "a_little_low": "Sit somewhere quiet and notice breath, sounds and body contact without demanding a kick or a particular outcome.", "okay": "Take two minutes to notice what is present — movement or stillness — then return to your day without repeatedly checking.", "good": "Create a short playlist for this stage. Include music that settles you and music that helps you feel like yourself.", "really_good": "Enjoy connection lightly: sing, speak or rest a hand on your belly if you want to. Bonding does not require a perfect response."}'::jsonb, 'Protect sleep where you can: dim lights earlier, keep the room comfortable and use pillows to support your preferred side-lying position.', 'Create a short playlist for this stage of pregnancy. Include songs for you, not only songs "for baby."', 'Invite your partner to feel movement if and when you want to. If the timing does not work, let it be light rather than disappointing.', 'You are learning a new language of sensations one day at a time.', 'If movement has become usual for you and then clearly decreases or changes, contact your care team rather than waiting for the next appointment.'),
  (26, 'second', 'Second-trimester graduation', 'Look how much you have carried already.', 'Close this trimester with reflection, practical preparation and pride.', 'You are approaching the third trimester. Your uterus, blood volume and daily physical workload have all grown, and your baby continues to gain weight and mature.', ARRAY['Stronger movement and a more prominent bump','More back, rib or pelvic discomfort','Heartburn, breathlessness with effort or sleep changes','Excitement, impatience, worry, or emotional neutrality']::text[], '{"focus": "Finish the trimester with a routine you can adapt, not outgrow.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Choose your favourite two movements from this trimester and perform them gently for 5 minutes total.", "steady": "Walk for 7 minutes, then complete one round of 10 chair sit-to-stands, 10 wall push-ups, 10 heel raises and 8 supported side steps per direction.", "feeling_good": "Warm up for 5 minutes. Complete 15 minutes of walking, swimming or stationary cycling, then one to two rounds of your favourite supported strength moves. Cool down for 5 minutes."}, "mood_adjustment": "Uncomfortable: choose mobility and comfort. Proud and energised: repeat favourites without chasing personal records. Apprehensive about the third trimester: note which options reliably help; these become your adaptable baseline.", "safety": "Use stable support when needed, breathe continuously and keep the effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling or anything that feels wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Make a third-trimester support plan: three easy meals, three snacks and one person or service you can call on when cooking feels hard.', 'Refresh your system for the next trimester: place bottles where you rest, work and leave home. Follow any personalised fluid advice from your clinician.', '{"heavy_day": "You do not need to feel ready for the third trimester today. Choose one immediate comfort and let the future remain outside this hour.", "a_little_low": "Complete this sentence honestly: \"In the next trimester, I need more help with...\" Save the answer without solving it tonight.", "okay": "Review one thing that supported you this trimester and one thing you want to change. Keep the reflection practical and kind.", "good": "Create a small time capsule: one photo, one voice note, one favourite memory and one thing you are proud of.", "really_good": "Mark the ending in a way that feels true — a meal, a walk, a private celebration or a conversation about how far you have come."}'::jsonb, 'Do a comfort audit: shoes, bras, sleep support, work setup and daily lifting. Change one thing now instead of waiting until discomfort is severe.', 'Create a second-trimester time capsule: one photo, one voice note, one favourite memory and one thing you are proud of.', 'Have a gentle planning conversation: appointments, help at home, birth education and how each of you communicates when anxious. You do not need to solve everything tonight.', 'You have carried this pregnancy for roughly six months. Pause before moving on. This trimester deserves its own ending.', 'Confirm upcoming appointments, glucose-screening follow-up if relevant, local vaccine recommendations, and when your care team wants you to begin monitoring movement more formally.')
on conflict (week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  priority = excluded.priority,
  journey = excluded.journey,
  what_you_may_notice = excluded.what_you_may_notice,
  move = excluded.move,
  nourish = excluded.nourish,
  hydration_goal = excluded.hydration_goal,
  reset = excluded.reset,
  care_for_yourself = excluded.care_for_yourself,
  your_corner = excluded.your_corner,
  support_moment = excluded.support_moment,
  celebrate_this_week = excluded.celebrate_this_week,
  for_your_care_team = excluded.for_your_care_team;
